  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is conclusive: five of the eight metabolites map to **Tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG hsa00380, FDR = 0.0, fold enrichment = 625), and four of those five additionally map to the **Tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD⁺ production** pathway (WP4210, FDR = 0.0, fold enrichment = 1175). L-Tryptophan (C00078) is the unambiguous upstream driver of this cluster, with KEGG reaction-graph BFS confirming two forward branches from it: (1) L-Tryptophan → 5-Hydroxy-L-tryptophan → Serotonin → N-Acetylserotonin → Melatonin (path length 4), and (2) L-Tryptophan → Kynurenine → 3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid → Quinolinic acid → NAD⁺ (path length 4). Serotonin (C00780) and 3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632) are verified members of both pathways via `query_pathway_membership`, and Quinolinic acid (C03722) is independently confirmed in Tryptophan metabolism, Nicotinate/nicotinamide metabolism, and the NAD⁺ biosynthesis modules. Melatonin (C01598) is additionally confirmed in Reactome's \"Serotonin and melatonin biosynthesis\" (R-HSA-209931).\n\nBiologically, these data point to a coordinated shift in tryptophan allocation between the kynurenine arm (producing the neurotoxic intermediate quinolinic acid and the NAD⁺ precursor) and the serotonin-melatonin arm. 3-Methyladenine (C00913), an autophagy and p53 pathway modulator, and O-phosphoethanolamine (C00346), a phosphoethanolamine cycle intermediate, fall outside the tryptophan pathways and may represent independent perturbed nodes. 4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid (C01127), a tricarboxylic acid cycle-variant metabolite, adds a glycolytic/energy-metabolism dimension.\n\nThe dual activation of both the kynurenine → quinolinic acid route and the serotonin → melatonin route suggests the treatment manipulates tryptophan flux at the point of tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase / indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase branching, with downstream consequences for neuroinflammation (via quinolinic acid), circadian and antioxidant signaling (via melatonin), and serotonergic neurotransmission. The simultaneous elevation of both terminal products indicates a broad, non-selective induction rather than a block at a single enzymatic step.",
